  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Calculate the points where the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ 4+6(y+2)^{2} & =36 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2} & =32 \\\\ 3(y+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{3} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,-2-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3})$ and $(0,-2+\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}).$",
